  "question": {
    "text": "If the probability that the random variable X takes the value $x$ is given by $P(X=x)=k(x+1) 3^{-x}$, $\\mathrm{x}=0,1,2,3 \\ldots \\ldots$, where k is a constant, then $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{X} \\geq 3)$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "4giNy",
      "text": "$\\frac{7}{27}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qM7qj",
      "text": "$\\frac{4}{9}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qV0bT",
      "text": "$\\frac{8}{27}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "whR5s",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{9}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\sum_{x=0}^{\\infty} k(x+1) 3^{-x}=1$<br/>$\\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{\\mathrm{k}}=1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{3}{3^2}+\\frac{4}{3^3}+$   ...(i)<br/>$\\frac{1}{3 \\mathrm{k}}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3^2}+\\frac{3}{3^3}+\\ldots$   ...(ii)<br/>(i)- (ii) $\\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{\\mathrm{k}}-\\frac{1}{3 \\mathrm{k}}=1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3^2}+\\ldots$<br/>$\\begin{aligned} & \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{k}=\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ & \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{x} \\geq 3)=1-\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{x}=0)-\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{x}=1)-\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{x}=2) \\\\ & =1-\\mathrm{k}\\left(1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{3}{9}\\right)=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
